      natives: []
    ]
  ]
)
```

```elixir
use AL
```

## Why effects exist

An AL transaction can change durable state atomically. Reading a file, making an HTTP request, or sending bytes over a socket happens outside that transaction and cannot be rolled back.

An effect records a request for host work. The request is dispatched only after its transaction commits. When the work finishes, its result is written back to the effect object in a new AL transaction.

```text
transaction -> effect -> host work -> transaction
```

Use an effect for one host operation. Use a workflow when later AL transactions depend on the results of earlier effects.

## Run an effect

This object exposes the built-in file-reading edge as an ordinary AL method.

```elixir
{:atomic, _} =
  run do
    defclass :file_reader,
      super: :object,
      redef: true,
      ivars: [path: []] do
      defmethod(:init, [self, args, self]) do
        get(args, :path, _)
        call_next_method(self, args, self)
      end

      defmethod(:read, [self, effect]) do
        get(self, :path, path)
        emit_effect(:file, :read, [path], effect)
      end
    end
  end
```

Create a file for the example, then ask a `:file_reader` to read it.

```elixir
path =
  Path.join(
    System.tmp_dir!(),
    "al_effects_livebook_#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}.txt"
  )

File.write!(path, "hello from an effect")

{:atomic, {bindings, _state}} =
  run do
    new(:file_reader, %{path: ^path}, reader)
    read(reader, effect)
  end

effect = bindings[:"$effect"]
AL.await_effect(effect)
```

`emit_effect(provider, operation, arguments, effect)` creates and returns an effect object. Once complete, its `outcome` is either `{:ok, value}` or `{:error, reason}`.

```elixir
run do
  get(^effect, :status, :completed)
  get(^effect, :outcome, outcome)
end
```

`AL.await_effect/2` is useful when Elixir needs to wait for a result, as this notebook does. It waits for a completion event rather than polling AL state. Inside AL, use the effect object from a later workflow transaction.

## Chain transactions with a workflow

A workflow is a sequence of explicit transactions. When a transaction emits an effect, the workflow waits for it to complete before running the next transaction.

```elixir
{:atomic, _} =
  run do
    defworkflow :read_text_file, [path], outputs: [contents] do
      transaction do
        new(:file_reader, %{path: path}, reader)
        read(reader, effect)
      end

      transaction do
        get(effect, :outcome, {:ok, contents})
      end
    end
  end
```

Start the workflow from Elixir and wait for its declared outputs.

```elixir
{:ok, workflow} = AL.workflow(:read_text_file, [path])
AL.await_workflow(workflow)
```

The first transaction commits the reader and its effect. The file edge performs the read. Effect completion starts the second transaction, which reads the outcome and completes the workflow.

Variables needed by later transactions, such as `effect`, are retained by the workflow. If a transaction emits several effects, the next transaction starts after all of them complete.

## Handle errors

Errors are values stored on the effect:

```text
{:error, reason}
```

A later workflow transaction can match that value and recover with ordinary AL logic. If the next transaction has no successful path for the outcome, the workflow becomes `:blocked` and retains the failure condition for inspection.

## Long-lived resources

File watches, TCP listeners, and TCP connections can produce many events. Effects start, stop, connect, listen, send, or close those resources. Later host events enter AL as fresh transactions that call ordinary methods such as `receive` or `accept` on the resource object.

You do not need a workflow around every incoming event. Define how the AL object responds to the event, and let the edge invoke it when something happens.

